<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Pope</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1688">1688</a>–<a href="/years/1744">1744</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Pope</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Pope was an English poet, translator and satirist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, a leading figure of Augustan literature in the early 18th century. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to Catholic parents, he was barred by the Test Acts from university and largely taught himself from the classical and English poets, while Pott disease, a tuberculosis of the spine, affected his health from the age of 12. His works include An Essay on Criticism (1711), The Rape of the Lock (1712–1717) and The Dunciad (1728–1743), and his translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> paid for his move in 1719 to a villa at Twickenham. Many of his lines entered common speech.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-pope.jpg" alt="Alexander Pope" width="330" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Dahl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Pope_by_Michael_Dahl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pope's father, also Alexander Pope, was a successful linen merchant in the Strand, and his mother was Edith Turner. Both were Catholic, and the Test Acts banned Catholics from teaching, attending a university, voting or holding public office. In 1700 the family moved to Popeswood in Binfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>, because of anti-Catholic sentiment and a statute barring Catholics from living within ten miles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. His formal education ended there; he read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a>, Juvenal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, Chaucer, Shakespeare and Dryden, and studied French, Italian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek poets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His Pastorals, published in 1709, brought instant fame, and An Essay on Criticism, published anonymously on 15 May 1711, was as well received. Around 1711 he joined Tory writers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonathan-swift">Jonathan Swift</a>, Thomas Parnell and John Arbuthnot in the Scriblerus Club, and he also befriended Joseph Addison and Richard Steele. The Rape of the Lock, a mock-epic about a quarrel between Arabella Fermor and Lord Petre, appeared in 1712 and in a revised version in 1714. His translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, published from 1715 to 1720 under a deal with the publisher Bernard Lintot worth 200 guineas a volume, was praised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-johnson">Samuel Johnson</a>, while Richard Bentley said it was not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>.</p><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> of 1725 and 1726 was shared with William Broome and Elijah Fenton, a collaboration he tried to conceal. His edition of Shakespeare of 1725 removed about 1,560 lines, and Lewis Theobald's pamphlet Shakespeare Restored made Theobald the first hero of The Dunciad, which appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 1728 and brought him enemies who pursued him with slander. An Essay on Man, in heroic couplets, was published between 1732 and 1734 and sets out to vindicate the ways of God to man. After 1738 he wrote little, revising The Dunciad, whose fourth book appeared in 1742, with Colley Cibber replacing Theobald as king of dunces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pope died at his villa on 30 May 1744, about eleven o'clock at night, having received the Last Rites of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> the day before. He was buried in the nave of St Mary's Church, Twickenham. Joseph Warton claimed a decade after his death that his style was not the most excellent form of the art, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> named him a chief influence while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wordsworth">William Wordsworth</a> thought his style too decadent. His reputation revived in the 20th century, and the Twickenham edition of his poems appeared in ten volumes between 1953 and 1967. Part of his grotto at Twickenham survives and is open to the public on some weekends.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander Pope” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In English George Chapman's version in fourteeners began to appear in 1598, <b>Alexander Pope</b>'s heroic couplets in 1715 and William Cowper's blank verse in 1791, and Matthew Arnold's lectures On Translating Homer (1861) set out the qualities a translator must render.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Later versions include Dacier's French prose of 1716, <b>Alexander Pope</b>'s of the 1720s, produced with Elijah Fenton and William Broome, and Vasily Zhukovsky's Russian of 1849.</blockquote><a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A little Learning is a dang'rous thing; Drink deep, or taste not the Pierian spring: There shallow draughts intoxicate the brain, And drinking largely sobers us again.&#8221;</p><footer>An Essay on Criticism (1711), part 2 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Works_of_Alexander_Pope_(1717)/Essay_on_Criticism" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Good-nature and good-sense must ever join; To err is humane, to forgive, divine.&#8221;</p><footer>An Essay on Criticism (1711), part 2 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Works_of_Alexander_Pope_(1717)/Essay_on_Criticism" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nay, fly to altars; there they'll talk you dead; For Fools rush in where Angels fear to tread.&#8221;</p><footer>An Essay on Criticism (1711), part 3 &#183; original &#183; of bad critics &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Works_of_Alexander_Pope_(1717)/Essay_on_Criticism" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
